# SETTINGS SCREEN

Version 1.0

---

# Purpose

The Settings Screen allows the athlete to personalize CAS while preserving a consistent training experience.

Settings exist to adapt the application to the athlete.

Not the opposite.

Every option should solve a real need.

If a setting exists only because it might be useful someday, it should not exist.

---

# Core Principle

Simplicity over customization.

The athlete should spend very little time inside Settings.

Training remains the primary activity.

---

# Primary Questions

The Settings Screen answers:

- How does CAS work for me?
- What information does CAS use?
- How do I control my training experience?

---

# Information Hierarchy

The Settings Screen follows this order:

1. Athlete Profile
2. Training Preferences
3. Recovery & Notifications
4. Audio & Accessibility
5. Privacy & Data
6. About CAS

---

# Athlete Profile

The athlete profile is the foundation of every recommendation.

Display:

Name

Age

Height

Weight

Primary sport

Experience level

Training objective

Dominant side

Available equipment

Preferred training duration

Preferred training days

Editing the profile should immediately influence future recommendations.

---

# Training Preferences

Examples:

Preferred units

Metric

Imperial

Default RPE display

Rest timer enabled

Automatic progression suggestions

Automatic exercise substitutions

Training reminders

Each preference should include a concise explanation.

---

# Recovery Preferences

Possible options:

Recovery reminders

Sleep reminders

Hydration reminders

Deload notifications

Recovery recommendations

Recovery settings should remain optional.

---

# Voice Guidance

Voice guidance can be enabled or disabled.

Possible options:

Announce next exercise

Announce load

Announce repetitions

Announce rest countdown

Announce session completion

Volume adjustment

Voice speed

Language

Voice guidance should always remain concise.

---

# Haptics

Allow independent control of:

Set validation

Rest countdown

Session completion

Warnings

The athlete may disable all haptics.

---

# Notifications

Notifications should remain useful.

Examples:

Today's training

Recovery reminder

Upcoming session

Training block completed

Missed session reminder

Never send motivational spam.

---

# Accessibility

Settings should include:

Dynamic Type compatibility

Reduced Motion support

High contrast mode

VoiceOver optimization

Large touch targets

These settings complement, but never replace, system accessibility settings.

---

# Appearance

CAS uses a single official Dark Theme.

No Light Theme.

No color themes.

No decorative skins.

The visual identity remains consistent.

---

# Units

Allow:

Kilograms

Pounds

Meters

Feet

Celsius

Fahrenheit

The athlete should never see mixed units.

---

# Language

Supported languages should be selectable.

Changing language should not require restarting the application.

---

# Privacy

Clearly explain:

What data is stored.

What data remains local.

What data is synchronized.

What data is shared.

Privacy information should remain understandable by non-technical users.

---

# Data Management

Possible actions:

Export training data

Import data

Backup

Restore

Delete local data

Delete account

Every destructive action requires confirmation.

---

# Explainability

The athlete may choose the level of explanation provided by CAS.

Suggested modes:

Minimal

Standard

Detailed

Examples:

Minimal

"Increase load next session."

Standard

"Increase load because the previous three sessions were completed successfully."

Detailed

Complete explanation of the decision model and contributing factors.

This setting should never affect the recommendation itself.

Only its explanation.

---

# AI Features

If AI-assisted features are available, they should be grouped here.

Examples:

Automatic coaching insights

Training summaries

Natural language explanations

Voice coaching

Future experimental features

Experimental features should always be clearly identified.

---

# Connected Devices

Future integrations may include:

Apple Health

Apple Watch

Garmin

Polar

WHOOP

Heart-rate sensors

Power meters

Connection status should remain simple.

---

# About CAS

Display:

Application version

Training Engine version

Database version

Explainability Engine version

Copyright

Licenses

Privacy policy

Terms of use

Support

This section remains informational.

---

# Layout

Recommended order:

Profile

↓

Training

↓

Recovery

↓

Voice & Accessibility

↓

Privacy

↓

About

Each section should remain compact.

Avoid very long scrolling.

---

# Typography

Section titles:

Heading 2

Option labels:

Body

Descriptions:

Caption

Values:

Body Medium

---

# Color Usage

Background:

Graphite Black

Interactive controls:

Primary Text

Selected state:

Performance Orange

Success:

Recovery Green

Warnings:

Warning Amber

Critical:

Critical Red

Settings should feel calm.

Not colorful.

---

# Motion

Allowed:

Standard navigation transitions

Toggle animations

Selection feedback

Not allowed:

Decorative transitions

Animated backgrounds

Repeated motion

Respect Reduced Motion.

---

# Accessibility

Every setting must be reachable using:

VoiceOver

Dynamic Type

Keyboard navigation (future)

Large touch targets

No setting should depend only on color.

---

# Error Handling

If a setting cannot be applied:

Explain why.

Offer recovery.

Never silently ignore the user's choice.

---

# Success Criteria

The athlete should:

Understand every setting.

Need very little documentation.

Be able to personalize CAS in less than two minutes.

---

# Do

Keep settings minimal.

Explain advanced options.

Respect platform conventions.

Preserve consistency.

---

# Do Not

Expose internal implementation.

Multiply cosmetic options.

Allow conflicting settings.

Add options "just in case."

---

# Final Principle

Settings exist to support training.

They should never become the athlete's primary destination.